Name: unrecognizedVoiceCommand

Type: message

Syntax: unrecognizedVoiceCommand <text>

Summary:
Sent to the current card of the default stack when the speech recognition
engine hears something but the transcribed text does not match any
registered voice command phrase. The raw transcript is passed as the
parameter, making it suitable for forwarding to an LLM or other
natural language processor.

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, Windows, Linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- Forward unrecognised speech to Claude for natural language handling
on unrecognizedVoiceCommand pText
    -- Send to your own LLM handler
    sendToAssistant pText
end unrecognizedVoiceCommand

Example:
-- Log what was heard for debugging
on unrecognizedVoiceCommand pText
    put "Heard but not matched: " & pText & return after field "Log"
end unrecognizedVoiceCommand

Example:
-- Blend commands and free-form input
on unrecognizedVoiceCommand pText
    -- Treat anything unmatched as a dictation input to the focused field
    if the focused object is a field then
        put pText after the text of the focused object
    end if
end unrecognizedVoiceCommand

Parameters:
text (string): The raw transcription of what was heard, as produced by
the speech recognition engine. This is the actual spoken words, not a
registered phrase — it may be a complete sentence, a question, or
anything else the user said.

Description:
<unrecognizedVoiceCommand> is dispatched whenever the engine transcribes
speech that does not match any phrase registered with
<registerVoiceCommand>. This lets you handle free-form spoken input
alongside structured voice commands — for example, forwarding the
transcript to ChatGPT or Claude for natural language processing, or
using it as dictation input.

Firing conditions:
- Always-on mode (no wake word set): fires for any unmatched utterance.
- Wake word mode, command window open: fires when the user speaks during
  the command window but nothing matches. The command window is also
  closed, as if the utterance consumed the window.
- Wake word mode, waiting for wake word: does NOT fire. Background speech
  that misses the wake word is silently ignored to avoid noise.

The message is sent to the current card of the default stack and bubbles
up the normal HyperXTalk message path.

References: registerVoiceCommand (command), voiceCommand (message),
startListening (command), setWakeWord (command)

Tags: speech, voice
